# Borderline sound board (834-0055) -- netlist converted from MAME
#
# beta-tester's netlist of the Borderline sound board, from the schematics in the
# Borderline Quest Owner's Manual (420-0613), sheets 6 and 7 of 7 -- the file's header
# lists each sound's trigger and the sheet grid squares it runs through. ICs are IC1-IC38
# on the sheet, written U1-U38 here. Two of MAME's notes about the sheet are carried: the
# transistors of the sound generators are drawn with base and collector swapped on the
# schematic, which MAME corrects (so this file is wired as MAME has it, not as drawn); and
# the MB4391 is a part MAME has no model of -- its file's MB4391 is 'a fake substitution',
# its behaviour guessed, though the pins it puts things on are the sheet's. The eight
# sound triggers arrive as ports from the logic board's LS374 latch; MAME's file gives
# the latch-to-trigger order as it wires the cabinet (D0 ANIMAL ... D7 GUN).
#
# Source: MAME mame0289 (commit f34f02505e32), src/mame/sega/nl_brdrline.cpp,
# NETLIST_START(brdrline). Licence CC0-1.0 by the file's own header (line 1),
# copyright-holders beta-tester (https://github.com/beta-tester) (line 2).
# https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mamedev/mame/mame0289/src/mame/sega/nl_brdrline.cpp
# Converted by tools/mame_netlist.py; checked by tools/build_board_nets.py.
#
# What the conversion means. MAME's netlists are written to be simulated, so this is a
# second reading of the sheet by someone else, not a read of it: designators and pin
# numbers are MAME's unless the notes say the sheet was read to correct them. Names on
# `label` lines are MAME's, which mostly follow the sheet's with Q for an overbar; `net`
# is used only for the supply rails. A `port` is a net that leaves what this file
# describes -- a signal from the CPU or the other board, a cabinet control, or a source
# MAME simulates with a stand-in rather than the circuit.
#
#   net NAME: PIN ...     a supply rail
#   label NAME: PIN ...   a net MAME names
#   wire PIN PIN ...      an unnamed connection
#   port NAME: PIN ...    a net that leaves this file (see above)
#   nc PIN ...            a pin with nothing attached
#   parallel PIN ...      outputs the board sums on one node by design
#   value REF VALUE       a passive's value, in the unit MAME's own macro uses
#
# PIN is DESIGNATOR.NUMBER. Diode pin 1 is the cathode; transistors keep B, C and E;
# MAME does not record which way round an electrolytic goes, so C pins are MAME's 1/2.
# The sheet numbers its ICs IC1, IC2 ...; they are written U1, U2 ... here, because the
# checker files every IC under a U designator.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Undone: what MAME does to simulate the board that the board does not do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# line 66: #define ENABLE_FRONTIERS 1 -> 0 (solver partitioning only)
# line 447: dropped NET_C(IC30.6, IC29.4) -- sheet 6 feeds IC29 pin 5 from IC30 pin 6, and pin 4 from pin 8
# line 448: dropped NET_C(IC30.8, IC29.5, R123.1) -- as above
# line 380: NET_C(IC27.7, IC27.6) -> nc -- MAME's UNUSED_OP_OUT_MINUS(), which ties an unused op-amp's output to its inverting input; the file calls it a hack
# line 381: NET_C(UNUSED_OP_REF, IC27.5) -> nc -- MAME's UNUSED_OP_PLUS(), which ties an unused op-amp input to a reference MAME adds; the file calls it a hack
